# PostgreSQL connection string. Prisma is the single source of truth for the schema.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/tara?schema=public"

# Direct (non-pooled) connection, used by `prisma migrate` when DATABASE_URL points
# at a connection pooler such as PgBouncer or Supabase's pooler.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/tara?schema=public"

# Default city used for the home screen when a visitor has no saved address yet.
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEFAULT_CITY_ID="city_manila"

# --- Authentication -----------------------------------------------------------
# Keys the HMAC over login codes and the hash of client-address fingerprints.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Rotating it invalidates every outstanding login code (sessions survive, since
# those are hashed with plain SHA-256 of a random token).
AUTH_SECRET="replace-me-with-openssl-rand-hex-32-at-least-32-chars"

# SMS delivery. With no provider configured, development prints codes to the
# server console; production refuses to start a login rather than pretend to
# send. Two adapters ship — see src/lib/auth/sms/ to add another.
#
# CONFIGURE ONE. If both are set, Semaphore wins: declaration order in
# src/lib/auth/sms/registry.ts is a precedence, not a fallback, and a failed
# send is never retried against the other gateway. /admin/health names the one
# in use. To move from Semaphore to PhilSMS, unset SEMAPHORE_API_KEY.
#
# --- Semaphore (semaphore.co) ------------------------------------------------
# Around ₱0.50–0.56 per message. Sender name is optional and unregistered names
# are rejected outright, so leave it unset until the application is approved.
# SEMAPHORE_API_KEY=""
# SEMAPHORE_SENDER_NAME=""
#
# --- PhilSMS (philsms.com) ---------------------------------------------------
# Around ₱0.35 per message with no minimum top-up, which is why it is here: on
# a deployment whose only SMS traffic is login codes, that difference is most
# of the bill.
#
# BOTH VARIABLES ARE REQUIRED. PhilSMS has no account-default sender, so a
# token on its own is not a gateway that half-works — it is one that fails on
# the first login. With only one set the deployment reads as unconfigured,
# which is the honest answer.
#   PHILSMS_API_TOKEN — app.philsms.com → Developers → API Tokens
#   PHILSMS_SENDER_ID — your approved Sender ID, max 11 alphanumeric characters
# PHILSMS_API_TOKEN=""
# PHILSMS_SENDER_ID=""
#
# Send one real message to confirm the account works, before trusting it:
#   npm run sms:send-one -- 09171234567
#
# Redirects sends to another host. For pointing at a local stub in
# development; IGNORED when NODE_ENV=production, because a variable that
# can redirect message delivery is a way to capture login codes.
# SEMAPHORE_ENDPOINT=""
# PHILSMS_ENDPOINT=""
#
# --- Signing in without paying for SMS yet -----------------------------------
# An ALLOWLIST of numbers that sign in with a fixed code and no text message,
# so a dry run does not need a prepaid gateway balance. Comma separated pairs
# of number:six-digit-code.
#
#   AUTH_TEST_NUMBERS="09171234567:123456,09181234568:654321"
#
# What it does NOT do, deliberately: it does not turn OTP off. A number that is
# not on this list takes the real path, and on a deployment with no gateway
# that still means refused — so this cannot be the way a stranger gets in as
# somebody else. The row it writes is a real row: same five-minute expiry, same
# single use, same five-attempt ceiling, same resend cooldown and request
# throttles. The only differences are which six digits are in it and that
# nothing was sent.
#
# UNLIKE SEMAPHORE_ENDPOINT this is honoured in production, because a dry run
# happens on a production build — that is what makes it a dry run. The price of
# that is noise: the login screen tells every visitor the deployment is in test
# mode and lists the masked numbers, and /admin/health reports it in red. A
# fixed code is a password that never changes, so unset this before you have
# real customers.
# AUTH_TEST_NUMBERS=""

# --- Web push -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Generate ONCE with `npm run push:keys` and keep the pair. Rotating it does not
# rotate a credential — it invalidates every existing push subscription, because
# each browser bound its subscription to the public key it was handed, and there
# is no migration path. Back the private key up somewhere that is not a laptop.
#
# With no keys set, push is simply absent: the switch does not appear, and any
# queued push deliveries stay PENDING until keys exist rather than being thrown
# away.
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=""
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=""
# A mailto: address or https: URL a push service operator can use to reach you
# about your traffic. The only contact path when Google or Mozilla decides your
# sending pattern looks like abuse, so make it one somebody reads.
# VAPID_SUBJECT="mailto:ops@your-domain.ph"

# --- Email, for account recovery ----------------------------------------------
# BOTH are required together, or email is treated as unconfigured: a key with no
# From address cannot send, and a From address with no key does nothing. The
# From domain must be verified with the provider — an unverified sender is the
# most common cause of mail that the API accepts and never delivers.
#
# With no provider set, recovery by email is simply absent: the profile screen
# says so, /recover says so, and support-assisted recovery through /admin is
# the only route. That is honest; pretending otherwise strands somebody who has
# already lost their number.
# RESEND_API_KEY=""
# EMAIL_FROM="TARA <no-reply@your-domain.ph>"
#
# Redirects mail to another host, for pointing at a local stub in development.
# IGNORED when NODE_ENV=production: a variable that can redirect delivery is a
# way to capture the codes that move an account.
# RESEND_ENDPOINT=""


# --- Login CAPTCHA ------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare Turnstile, free at any volume. What it protects is MONEY, not
# access: the boundary on an account is the code sent to a phone somebody
# holds, and a CAPTCHA adds nothing to that. What it stops is a script spending
# a peso of yours per request, a few thousand times.
#
# BOTH are required together. With only one set the login screen deliberately
# behaves as though no CAPTCHA were configured — a secret with no site key
# renders no widget, so nobody could produce a token and nobody could sign in,
# and locking out every customer is a worse failure than letting a few extra
# requests through to the rate limits.
#
# Get a pair at https://dash.cloudflare.com → Turnstile. Use the "Managed"
# widget: it resolves silently for a normal browser, which matters on a low-end
# handset over patchy mobile data.
#
# Note the trade-off before switching this on: the first login step is
# otherwise built to work before the page has finished loading, and a CAPTCHA
# cannot be — it is JavaScript by construction. With a pair set, signing in
# requires JavaScript, and the screen says so.
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=""
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=""
#
# Redirects verification to another host, for pointing at a local stub in
# development. IGNORED when NODE_ENV=production: a variable that can redirect
# the check is a way to make every check pass.
# TURNSTILE_ENDPOINT=""

# --- Backups ------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS FIRST: the real backup is a feature of wherever the database is
# hosted. Point-in-time recovery on a managed Postgres restores to the second
# and survives this machine catching fire. TURN IT ON. Nothing in this
# repository replaces it.
#
# What `npm run db:backup` adds is a portable dump you can take off the
# platform — the answer to "the provider suspended our account" and to "we are
# moving hosts". And `npm run db:restore-check` restores it into a scratch
# database and checks that the ledger still adds up, because a dump nobody has
# restored is a hope rather than a backup.
#
# Declare which it is, so the console stops guessing:
#   host   — the provider does it. The console says so, and says plainly that
#            this application cannot see whether it is true.
#   script — the schedule below is the strategy. The console then warns when a
#            backup is older than a day and a half.
# BACKUP_STRATEGY="host"
#
# A long passphrase. WITHOUT IT the dump is written in plaintext, and a dump is
# every customer's phone number and home address in one file that is designed
# to be copied elsewhere. WITH IT, keep the passphrase somewhere that survives
# the loss of this machine: a dump you cannot decrypt is not a backup.
# BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=""
#
# A daily dump at 02:00 Manila time, keeping a fortnight, then proving it:
#   0 18 * * *  cd /srv/tara && npm run db:backup -- --keep 14
#   30 18 * * * cd /srv/tara && npm run db:restore-check
# Both need the Postgres 16 client tools (pg_dump, pg_restore, psql, createdb)
# on whichever machine runs them — which does not have to be the application
# server, and often should not be.

# --- Reaching a person --------------------------------------------------------
# Support has two paths and they are NOT alternatives.
#
#   Signed in  → a ticket. A thread in the app, with a record on both sides and
#                a notification when somebody replies. This needs no
#                configuration and already works.
#   Signed out → whatever is below. THIS IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS MOST, and the
#                reason is the person it is for: somebody who lost the SIM, or
#                whose code never arrives, or whose number now belongs to
#                somebody else. They cannot raise a ticket. For them an in-app
#                form is a locked door with a note on it.
#
# Set at least ONE of the three. All are optional, nothing is invented, and
# /admin/health reports "No public channel" in red until one is set — the only
# gap in this application that costs somebody their account rather than a
# feature.
#
# A mobile number, Philippine format. Shown as a `tel:` link on /help and
# /recover. Validated on the way in, because a mistyped number renders as a
# link that silently dials nothing.
# SUPPORT_PHONE="09171234567"
#
# SUPPORT_EMAIL="help@example.com"
#
# A Facebook page. In the Philippines a Page inbox is how most people expect
# to reach a business, it costs nothing to run, and somebody with no load on
# their phone can still send a message. https only.
# SUPPORT_FACEBOOK="https://facebook.com/yourpage"
#
# Free text, shown under the channels. Say the truth: "9am-9pm, every day" is
# useful, and so is "weekdays only, we reply the next morning". A number nobody
# answers is worse than no number.
# SUPPORT_HOURS="9am-9pm, every day"

# --- The map on the store form ------------------------------------------------
# Optional, and there is nothing to do here for a normal deployment.
#
# /admin/stores has a map for placing a shop's pin, and the coordinates matter:
# every delivery fee from that shop is measured from them, so a transposed pair
# does not fail — it charges the wrong money forever. Tiles come from
# OpenStreetMap by default: free, no account, no key, and better coverage of
# barangay streets than some paid providers. Their tile policy permits light
# use, and a console form is about as light as it gets.
#
# THAT HAS NOW CHANGED, so set these before real volume. Live tracking renders
# a map on a CUSTOMER screen for every order in transit, which is not light use
# by anybody's reading, and OSM's policy is then to self-host or buy tiles.
# `/admin/health` warns while the default is in use. A Leaflet URL template —
# it must contain {z}, {x} and {y}, or the app ignores it and falls back rather
# than repeating one tile across the world.
# MAP_TILE_URL="https://tiles.example.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"
#
# Whoever the tiles come from almost certainly requires crediting, and for
# OpenStreetMap it is a licence condition rather than a courtesy.
# MAP_TILE_ATTRIBUTION="© Example Tiles"
#
# NOT a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable, deliberately: those are inlined into the browser
# bundle at build time, so one would freeze whatever was set during
# `docker build` and ignore this file. It is read on the server and passed to
# the component as a prop.

# --- Address search on the store form -----------------------------------------
# Also optional, and it works with nothing set here.
#
# The map has a "search for the address" box so somebody with a shop name and a
# street does not have to pan across the archipelago. It goes to Nominatim,
# OpenStreetMap's own geocoder, THROUGH THIS SERVER rather than from the
# browser — their policy asks for a User-Agent naming the application, which a
# browser cannot send, and caps requests at one a second, which is a throttle
# worth having in one place rather than in every operator's tab.
#
# Set this to "off" to switch it off entirely. The box then does not appear at
# all, rather than failing when somebody uses it — worth doing if this
# deployment should not make outbound calls.
# GEOCODER_URL="off"
#
# Or point it at a paid provider or a self-hosted Nominatim, in the same
# Nominatim /search shape.
# GEOCODER_URL="https://nominatim.example.ph/search"
#
# RECOMMENDED for a real deployment. Nominatim's policy asks for a way to
# reach whoever is making the requests before they resort to blocking. It goes
# into the User-Agent and Nominatim's own `email` parameter, and it is sent to
# them and nobody else.
# GEOCODER_CONTACT="ops@yourdomain.ph"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Taking money in advance
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cash on delivery needs no configuration and always works. These three switch
# on the PREPAID rail: the customer sends the total in their own wallet app —
# GCash, Maya, a bank — puts the order number in the note, and types the
# reference back into TARA. Somebody with access to the receiving account
# checks it at /admin/payments and confirms, and only then does the order reach
# the shop.
#
# ALL THREE OR NONE. A checkout that offers a transfer with no account to send
# it to takes an order nobody can pay, so a half-filled configuration switches
# the rail OFF rather than guessing — the same rule the CAPTCHA and the email
# provider follow. /admin/health says which one is missing.
#
# Why this rather than a payment provider: a provider account needs DTI or SEC
# registration and BIR registration, which is work outside this codebase, and
# it charges per transaction against a margin that is one delivery fee. This
# needs a phone number. What it costs is a person's attention, once per order,
# so it does not scale past the volume one person can check — there is a seam
# for a provider rail in src/lib/payments/rails/ when it starts hurting.
#
# The brand is a LABEL, not a branch: the same build serves Maya or a bank.
# PAYMENT_TRANSFER_LABEL="GCash"
#
# The name on the receiving account, so a customer can check before sending.
# PAYMENT_TRANSFER_ACCOUNT_NAME="Juan Dela Cruz"
#
# The number or account they send to. Displayed to the customer, never parsed.
# PAYMENT_TRANSFER_ACCOUNT_NUMBER="0917 123 4567"
